     User annotations are described in pkg_create(1).  The following annota‐
     tions are automatically inserted during package creation and installa‐
     tion:

     @@aarrcchh _a_r_c_h_e_s
             List of architectures for which this package is intended.  This
             corresponds to --AA _a_r_c_h_e_s of pkg_create(1)

     @@ccoommmmeenntt ppkkggppaatthh==ppaatthh ffttpp==yyeess//nnoo
             Historical accident.  This specific comment encodes the actual --DD
             _F_U_L_L_P_K_G_P_A_T_H, and --DD _F_T_P arguments to pkg_create(1).

     @@ddeeppeenndd _p_k_g_p_a_t_h:_p_k_g_s_p_e_c:_d_e_f_a_u_l_t
             Record a dependency declared using the option --PP of
             pkg_create(1).

     @@ddiiggiittaall--ssiiggnnaattuurree _s_i_g_n_i_f_y_2:_d_a_t_e:_e_x_t_e_r_n_a_l
             Record the date of signature of a package, synthesized by
             pkg_add(1) from signify(1) output.

     @@lliinnkk _n_a_m_e
             Added after a file entry by ppaacckkaaggee to record that the entry is
             actually a hard link.

     @@llooccaallbbaassee _b_a_s_e
             Used internally to record the settings of --LL option.

     @@nnaammee _p_k_g_n_a_m_e
             Set the name of the package.  This name is potentially different
             than the name of the file it came in, and is used when keeping
             track of the package for later deinstallation.  pkg_create(1)
             will derive this field from the package file name.

     @@ooppttiioonn _n_a_m_e
             Some options are automatically inserted/modified by the package
             tools:

             _a_l_w_a_y_s_-_u_p_d_a_t_e
                     pkg_create(1) will complete the option line with a sha256
                     digest of the whole ppaacckkiinngg--lliisstt encoded in base64.

             _f_i_r_m_w_a_r_e
                     Set by fw_update(8) to trigger firmware-specific han‐
                     dling.  In particular, firmware is hidden from normal up‐
                     dates.

             _m_a_n_u_a_l_-_i_n_s_t_a_l_l_a_t_i_o_n
                     Record that a package has been explicitly installed by
                     the user, and not as a result of a dependency look-up.
                     Refer to pkg_add(1)'s --aa option for details.

     @@sshhaa    Added after a file entry by pkg_create(1) to record the files's
             cryptographic checksum, as a sha256 digest encoded in base64.

     @@ssiiggnneerr
             Record the signing key for a package, synthesized by pkg_add(1)
             from signify(1) output.

     @@ssiizzee   Added after a file entry by pkg_create(1) to record a file size.

     @@ssyymmlliinnkk _n_a_m_e
             Added after a file entry by pkg_create(1) to record that the en‐
             try is actually a symbolic link.

     @@uurrll    Original location of the package, synthesized by pkg_add(1) dur‐
             ing installation.

     @@ttss _t_i_m_e_s_t_a_m_p
             Added after a file entry to record the actual file timestamp, in‐
             stead of storing it as tarball meta-info.  The package tools read
             and process that annotation correctly, whereas tar(1) will only
             see files dated from the epoch.

     @@vveerrssiioonn _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Record a global (system) version number for the package.  This is
             built adding together --VV options from pkg_create(1), and triggers
             updates when it changes.

     @@wwaannttlliibb _l_i_b_s_p_e_c
             Record a library requirement declared using the option --WW of
             pkg_create(1).

     Note that most of these annotations cannot be added manually, as
     pkg_create(1) will error out.

PPAACCKKAAGGEE SSIIGGNNAATTUURREESS
     All information within a package is checksummed, using SHA256 since
     OpenBSD 4.4.  During creation and installation, meta-information, such as
     file owners and permissions, are also checked: any important stuff that
     isn't recorded in the ppaacckkiinngg--lliisstt is an error.

     Starting with OpenBSD 6.1, signify(1) --zzSS gzip(1) header signatures are
     the only supported format.  This allows for ‘just-in-time’ signature
     checking, as the binary data is checked in 64K bytes long chunks.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     pkg_add(1), pkg_create(1), pkg_info(1), pkg_sign(1), packages(7),
     packages-specs(7)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     Packages are valid gzip'ed ustar archives that can be extracted using
     tar(1).  In particular, hardlink names should be valid, and all items
     will extract to different names.  However, it may be a bit difficult to
     make sense of the package contents without peeking at the ppaacckkiinngg--lliisstt.

